WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.730
SPEAKER: To get started with
the Cloud Natural Language API

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.670
for Go, you'll need
a GCP project, which

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.960
you can set up in the console.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.670
In your project, enable
the Google Natural Language

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.800
API for that project.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.850
Then create a service account
and download the private key

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.130
as JSON.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.730
You need these credentials
to access Google Cloud APIs,

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:39.530
so keep them secure and out
of your code and public repos.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.810
To access the credentials
from your project,

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.480
go to the terminal and set
an environment variable

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.130
to the path of your
service account.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.430
Now install the client library.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.660
If you use Cloud
Shell in the console,

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.230
you can skip this step since
the required dependencies

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.610
are already included.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:02.700
Make a new file or
open an existing file.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.710
I'm using VS Code, but you can
use whatever IDE you prefer.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.920
Import the Google Cloud
client library as well as

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.340
some other packages you need.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.810
Then instantiate
a language client.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.680
If there is an error in
instantiating it, handle it.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.580
For the purposes
of this example,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.660
I'm just going to log it.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.713
Provide some text to analyze.

00:01:39.713 --> 00:01:41.630
I'm going to go with the
classic example here.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:56.173
Call AnalyzeSentiment passing
the context, a document object,

00:01:56.173 --> 00:01:57.215
and the type of encoding.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.960
The document comprises
a source of content,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.410
which, in this case, is
the text "Hello, world!"

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:09.259
and a type of content,
which is plain text.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.960
Include some error handling.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:18.600
Again, for now, I'm just
going to log the error.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.630
If there is no error, the
AnalyzeSentiment function

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.060
returns an analyze
sentiment response.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.420
Analyze sentiment response has
three properties-- document

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.980
sentiment, the overall
sentiment of the input document,

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.890
language, which is the language
of the text, and sentences,

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:39.690
which is the sentiment for all
the sentences in the document.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:44.130
In this example, I'm going to
focus on document sentiment.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:48.120
Log if the sentiment score
is positive or negative.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.820
Score is a sentiment
score between negative one

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.100
for negative sentiment
and positive one

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.820
for positive sentiment.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.560
OK, go ahead and run the code.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.210
And there you go.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.780
You just sent your first request
to the Natural Language API.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.020
But there is more that the Cloud
Natural Language API can do,

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.800
like analyzing syntax
and annotating text.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.020
To find out more about
the client libraries,

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.990
consult natural language basics,
or work through the sentiment

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:20.750
analysis tutorial, check
out the Cloud documentation.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:24.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

